    videoUrl: "",
    content: `PERCENTAGES & STATISTICS

PERCENTAGE FORMULAS:
Percent = (Part / Whole) × 100
Part = (Percent / 100) × Whole

PERCENT CHANGE:
% Change = ((New - Old) / Old) × 100

Example: Price increases by 15% each year starting at $10:
After 1 year: $10 × 1.15 = $11.50
After t years: $10 × (1.15)^t

DISCOUNT:
30% off means you pay 70%: new price = 0.70p

MEASURES OF CENTER:
- Mean = sum of all values / number of values
- Median = middle value when sorted (even: average of two middles)
- Mode = most frequent value

Example: Find median of {3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12}
Middle values are 7 and 8 → Median = (7+8)/2 = 7.5

STANDARD DEVIATION:
Measures spread. A higher SD means more spread out from the mean.

SURVEYS & SAMPLING:
Key rule: A random sample can only be generalized to the population it was drawn from.
- 100 students surveyed, 40% want a library → you can conclude 40 students IN THE SURVEY wanted a library. You CANNOT conclude 40% of all students do.

PERCENTAGES OF PERCENTAGES:
20% of 30% = 0.20 × 0.30 = 0.06 = 6%

WEIGHTED AVERAGES:
Mean of 7 boys = 67 inches → total = 7 × 67 = 469 inches
If one boy is 74 inches, remaining 6 total = 469 - 74 = 395
Mean of remaining 6 = 395/6 ≈ 65.8 inches`,
