    content: `MAIN IDEA & INFERENCE

MAIN IDEA QUESTIONS:
The main idea is NOT just what the first paragraph says — it covers the ENTIRE passage.
A good main idea answer is:
✓ Broad enough to cover the whole passage
✓ Not too broad (doesn't go beyond what the passage covers)
✓ Directly supported by the text

WRONG answer traps:
✗ Too narrow (only covers one paragraph)
✗ Too broad (adds ideas not in the passage)
✗ Contradicts the passage

INFERENCE QUESTIONS ("It can be reasonably inferred..."):
Inferences must be STRONGLY SUPPORTED by the text. If you have to stretch or guess, it's wrong.
The correct inference is one that must be true given what the passage says.

EVIDENCE QUESTIONS ("Which option gives the best evidence for the answer to the previous question?"):
The cited lines must DIRECTLY SUPPORT the previous answer — not just mention related ideas.

STEP-BY-STEP APPROACH:
1. Read the question carefully
2. Go back to the passage and find the relevant section
3. Eliminate answers that contradict the passage
4. Eliminate answers that go beyond what the passage says
5. Choose the answer most directly supported by the text

FUNCTION/PURPOSE QUESTIONS ("The main function of lines X–Y is..."):
Ask: why did the author include this? Common purposes:
- To provide context/background
- To support a claim with evidence
- To introduce a topic
- To contrast with another idea
- To engage the reader

SAT TIP: Never bring in outside knowledge. All answers must come from the passage itself.`,
