    content: `ABOUT THE DIGITAL SAT

WHAT IS THE SAT?
The SAT is a standardized test most U.S. colleges use as one factor in admissions decisions. The current version is fully digital, taken on a laptop or tablet through the College Board's "Bluebook" app, either at a school or an official testing center.

OVERALL STRUCTURE
The Digital SAT has two sections, always in this order:
1. Reading and Writing — two modules, 32 minutes each, 27 questions per module (54 total), 64 minutes total
2. A short break (about 10 minutes)
3. Math — two modules, 35 minutes each, 22 questions per module (44 total), 70 minutes total

Total testing time is about 2 hours and 14 minutes — much shorter than the old paper SAT.

ADAPTIVE TESTING — HOW IT WORKS
Each section (Reading & Writing, and Math) is "multistage adaptive": everyone gets the same Module 1. How well you do on Module 1 determines whether Module 2 is the harder version or the easier version.
- Strong Module 1 performance → harder Module 2 → access to the highest possible scores
- Weaker Module 1 performance → easier Module 2 → capped maximum score
This means Module 1 matters enormously — there's no "save your energy for later," since underperforming early limits your ceiling for the rest of the section.

SCORING
- Each section (Reading & Writing, Math) is scored from 200–800
- Total composite score ranges from 400–1600
- There is NO penalty for guessing, so always answer every question — leaving a question blank guarantees zero credit, while guessing gives you a chance
- Every question within a module counts the same toward your raw score, regardless of difficulty — but harder questions show up more often in a harder Module 2, which raises your scoring ceiling

KEY DIFFERENCES FROM THE OLD PAPER SAT
- Much shorter reading passages — short standalone passages (roughly 25–150 words) each paired with ONE question, instead of long passages with multiple questions
- Built-in graphing calculator (Desmos) available for the entire Math section
- No more "no calculator" math module — calculator use is allowed throughout
- Shorter overall test (about 2 hours vs. nearly 3 hours on paper)
- Scores come back faster than the paper test

WHAT THE SAT IS ACTUALLY TESTING
The SAT isn't a test of memorized facts — it doesn't test specific historical dates or science trivia. It's designed to test:
- Reading comprehension and the ability to interpret evidence (including data in charts/graphs)
- Command of standard written English (grammar, punctuation, sentence structure)
- Core math skills built up through algebra, advanced math, problem-solving/data analysis, and geometry/trigonometry

SAT TIP: Because the test is adaptive, your Module 1 strategy should be: work carefully and accurately rather than rushing. A few careless mistakes in Module 1 can lock you into an easier (lower-ceiling) Module 2 even if you would have aced harder questions.`,
